282
00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:07.380
It was as if time had stopped entirely,

283
00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:10.320
as though the world itself had paused in

284
00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:11.560
that one fleeting moment.

285
00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:14.740
I came here to tell you everything, Nastenka,

286
00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:18.460
feeling like that single sensation, that single overwhelming

287
00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:21.060
feeling, had taken hold of me forever.

288
00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:23.820
It was as if one minute of my

289
00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:26.420
life had stretched out endlessly, as though it

290
00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:30.440
could last for all eternity, and everything else—the

291
00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:33.040
entire world—had come to a halt.

292
00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:36.160
When I woke, it was as if some

293
00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:39.200
old forgotten melody, one I had once known

294
00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:42.120
so well long ago, but had since lost

295
00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:44.560
in the recesses of memory, had returned to

296
00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:44.740
me.

297
00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:48.360
It was a tune so sweet, so achingly

298
00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:50.900
familiar, that it seemed to echo deep in

299
00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:51.440
my heart.

300
00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:54.120
And I felt as though that melody had

301
00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:56.780
been waiting there all along, knocking at my

302
00:11:56.780 --> 00:11:58.680
soul, yearning to be heard.

303
00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:02.440
And now, at last, it had broken through.

304
00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:05.460
Oh, my goodness, my goodness!

305
00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:09.580
Nastenka interrupted, her voice cutting through my reverie.

306
00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:11.980
What on earth are you talking about?

307
00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:13.640
I don't understand a word of it!

308
00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:17.720
Her bluntness startled me, and I faltered, my

309
00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:18.560
hopes dimming.

310
00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:21.400
Yet there was still a faint, lingering hope

311
00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.120
hidden in my voice as I tried to

312
00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:23.600
explain.

313
00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:28.160
Ah, Nastenka, I said plaintively, I was just

314
00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:30.620
trying to describe to you that strange, overwhelming

315
00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:31.200
impression.

316
00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:33.480
Stop it, hush!

317
00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:35.840
she said suddenly, and in an instant I

318
00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:37.060
could see that she had guessed.

319
00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:40.300
Her sharp intuition had uncovered what I hadn't

320
00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:41.580
