439
00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:18.440
She began to repeat her address to me,

440
00:17:18.740 --> 00:17:21.099
her voice hurried and yet soft, as though

441
00:17:21.099 --> 00:17:22.720
she needed to make sure I would remember

442
00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:23.540
every word.

443
00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:26.740
Her insistence made me feel a strange pang

444
00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:29.820
of sadness, a deep longing to reassure her,

445
00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:31.920
to promise her more than I could.

446
00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:36.420
Then quite suddenly her mood shifted, she became

447
00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:39.500
unusually tender, almost maternal in the way she

448
00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:42.220
looked at me, her concern for me clear

449
00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:44.500
in every glance, every movement.

450
00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:48.560
She listened attentively as I spoke, nodding slightly

451
00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:50.200
as though she wanted me to believe she

452
00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:51.360
was truly hearing me.

453
00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:54.520
Yet when I asked her a question, she

454
00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:57.900
faltered, her silence spoke louder than any words

455
00:17:57.900 --> 00:17:58.560
might have.

456
00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:01.460
Her confusion deepened and she turned her head

457
00:18:01.460 --> 00:18:03.300
away, unable to meet my eyes.

458
00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:07.220
I leaned in slightly, trying to understand what

459
00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:07.800
was happening.

460
00:18:08.540 --> 00:18:10.880
When she finally turned back toward me, I

461
00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:13.700
saw it, tears glistening in her eyes.

462
00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:17.200
She was crying, though she was trying desperately

463
00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:17.960
to hide it.

464
00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:22.360
Nastenka, I said softly, alarmed by her sudden

465
00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:22.720
shift.

466
00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:23.880
